WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.041 --> 00:00:01.167
So your printer requested

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:04.337
that your project have bleed,
and at this point you're feeling

00:00:04.462 --> 00:00:05.463
one of two things.

00:00:05.463 --> 00:00:07.215
One, fear
that your printer is involved

00:00:07.215 --> 00:00:10.635
in some sort of cult,
and two - straight up confusion.

00:00:10.635 --> 00:00:13.096
Well, I have good news.
We don't want to cut you.

00:00:13.346 --> 00:00:14.514
We want to cut your paper.

00:00:14.514 --> 00:00:16.016
So I am here to clear this up

00:00:16.016 --> 00:00:17.767
and explain to you
what bleed is.

00:00:17.767 --> 00:00:19.811
Bleed in printing means
that you allow your project

00:00:19.811 --> 00:00:23.565
to extend past or bleed past
where you want your project cut.

00:00:23.648 --> 00:00:25.483
This allows your printer
some cushion room

00:00:25.483 --> 00:00:26.651
when cutting your project.

00:00:26.651 --> 00:00:29.029
Now, here are two examples
of the exact same job;

00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:30.905
one with bleed and one without.

00:00:30.905 --> 00:00:31.448
The project on

00:00:31.448 --> 00:00:34.451
the left has no bleed
and the one on the right does.

00:00:34.451 --> 00:00:35.994
Sometimes
during the cutting process

00:00:35.994 --> 00:00:38.371
your project can shift
one millimeter or so.

00:00:38.496 --> 00:00:40.749
Now, that doesn't
sound like a lot; however,

00:00:40.749 --> 00:00:42.459
if your project doesn't
have bleed,

00:00:42.459 --> 00:00:44.794
that one millimeter
can become quite noticeable.

00:00:44.794 --> 00:00:47.422
Now, we don't want that,
and we know you don't, either.

00:00:47.464 --> 00:00:48.214
To avoid this,

00:00:48.214 --> 00:00:49.632
we ask that our customers
include

00:00:49.632 --> 00:00:52.635
one eighth of an inch of bleed
on every edge of their project.

00:00:52.677 --> 00:00:54.387
This allows for plenty of room
to make sure

00:00:54.387 --> 00:00:56.681
your project comes out
exactly how you want it.

00:00:56.681 --> 00:00:58.892
Now, the easiest way to add
lead to an image

00:00:58.892 --> 00:01:00.310
is to simply make your image

00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:02.353
slightly larger
so that the new edges

00:01:02.353 --> 00:01:04.689
of the image
extend past the cut lines.

00:01:04.689 --> 00:01:06.733
This will ensure
a clean end result,

00:01:06.733 --> 00:01:09.277
with no ugly white lines
on any of the edges.

00:01:09.319 --> 00:01:10.904
If you want more helpful
content like this,

00:01:10.904 --> 00:01:12.614
make sure you subscribe
to our YouTube channel.

00:01:12.614 --> 00:01:14.115
Thank you so much for watching,
and we'll see you

00:01:14.115 --> 00:01:14.866
guys next time.

